:class:`~icalendar.cal.component_factory.ComponentFactory` no longer creates component classes for names that are not valid iCalendar tokens (:rfc:`5545#section-3.6`), so invalid component names are rejected when parsing iCalendar, jCal, and xCal. jCal component names must also be lowercase (:rfc:`7265#section-3.3`). The value of a ``BEGIN`` line was kept verbatim and used as the XML element name in :meth:`~icalendar.cal.component.Component.to_xcal`, so a name containing ``<`` or ``>`` injected elements into the xCal output and produced XML that is not well-formed. An invalid name now raises :exc:`~icalendar.error.ICalParsingError`, :exc:`~icalendar.error.JCalParsingError`, or :exc:`~icalendar.error.XCalParsingError`. :func:`~icalendar.parser.validate_token` now raises the new :exc:`~icalendar.error.InvalidToken`, a subclass of :exc:`~icalendar.error.InvalidCalendar`, and rejects non-ASCII characters in names, as :rfc:`5545#section-3.1` allows only ASCII letters, digits, and hyphens. I used AI to assist me with this change. @alhudz
